.TH CHTTPSVR_REQ_PEER_CERT_SUBJECT 3 "2026-10-01" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
chttpsvr_req_peer_cert_subject \- give the subject of the verified client certificate
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/chttpserver.h>

const char *chttpsvr_req_peer_cert_subject(const chttpsvr_req *req);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Returns the subject of the client certificate of the request as an RFC
2253 distinguished name, which lists its attributes from the most
specific to the least specific, for example

    CN=alice,OU=staff,O=Example

Every byte above 0x7F, every control character and every character
that RFC 2253 reserves is escaped with a backslash, so the string is
printable ASCII and a name crafted by a client cannot pass for
another one; an organization "Example, Inc." reads as
O=Example\e, Inc.  Compare the subject as a whole string, because a
substring of a subject can come from any of its attributes.

The subject is returned only when chttpsvr_req_peer_cert_verified(3)
reports true for req.  It belongs to the connection, so every request
on one keep-alive connection gets the same text, and a resumed TLS
session gets the subject verified by the handshake that established
it.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
req  The request handle.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
A NUL-terminated string that stays valid for the lifetime of the
request; do not free it.

NULL in every case where chttpsvr_req_peer_cert_verified(3) reports
false (a plaintext connection, a server with no ca_bundle_path, a
client that presented no certificate, and a NULL req), and also NULL
when an allocation fails; call chttpsvr_req_peer_cert_verified(3) to
tell the two apart.
.fi
.SH THREAD SAFETY
.nf
As with every other request accessor, call it only on the thread that
runs the handler or the middleware of req.
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
#include <ccollections/chttpserver.h>
#include <string.h>

static void handler(chttpsvr_req *req, chttpsvr_resp *resp, void *ctx)
{
    (void)ctx;
    const char *who = chttpsvr_req_peer_cert_subject(req);
    if (!who || strcmp(who, "CN=billing,O=Example") != 0) {
        chttpsvr_resp_set_status(resp, CHTTP_STATUS_FORBIDDEN);
        return;
    }
    chttpsvr_resp_write_str(resp, "ok");
}
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR chttpsvr_req_peer_cert_verified (3),
.BR chttpsvr_req_peer_cert_der (3),
.BR chttpsvr_req_peer_cert_sha256 (3)
